A||N||16||FieroJoe||New Aluminum Water Pump:  Dex-cool??||||5||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||12-27-2001||12:52 AM||||I just bought a new, lifetime warranty aluminum water pump for my V6 project engine today from autozone, and the salesman said that extended life coolant was "strongly recommended", it said it on the screen as well.  I would assume the extended life coolant that it is referring to would be Dex-Cool.  Now, is this completely necessary?  Seeing as Fiero coolant systems should be flushed regularly, and this stuff is expensive, I won't convert my system over to it unless it is completely necessary.  Is it possible anyways to convert to dex cool?  Are they just trying to get me to buy this $7 a gallon coolant?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierojoesig2.gif">  <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||12-27-2001||01:32 AM||||Just used distilled water and a good ethylene glycol. Dex-cool isn't worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||12-27-2001||03:10 AM||||someone might have more info about this<P>i know regular antifreeze contains silica <BR>and the dexcool doesn't.<P>It might be possible that the silica might harm the aluminum on the waterpump and cause the bearings to go bad.<P>You can convert to dexcool, but the only thing you have to do is flush your system clean.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||12-27-2001||03:15 AM||||No matter what coolant you use, it should always be flushed and refilled with distilled water and some redline water wetter at least every 2 years.. You and your car will be much happier.<P>I think one of the main benefits of dex-cool is that it helps stop corrosion on an alloy head/iron block engine.. so the dex-cool recommendation kinda makes sense.  I could be wrong, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_sig_pic_1.jpg">        <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-27-2001||07:21 AM||||I assume you're talking about the 'orange' 'long life' coolant.<P>Good stuff, WELL worth it!<P>It will gum up your engine far less.<P>I second the recomendation for RedLine's Water Wetter as well.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dumba$$||12-27-2001||10:51 AM||||a friend run the dex-crap in his sho and it ended up running 9* hotter  so he whitched back and threw some water wetter in there and ended up running 17*cooler than with the dex-junk<BR>thats the only exp. ive had with that and it didnt impress me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE <BR>98 acura cl 3.0<P>ever notice that people that own a fiero, either have, or have had other fieros?||||reg||13
Z||000006||FieroJoe||12-27-2001||11:06 AM||||I was definatly going to use water wetter, have heard a lot of great things about it.  I plan on flushing the system every year anyways, so if I use high quality ethylene glycol (green) coolant and water wetter, flush it every year, should I have problems with the water pump?||||reg||1
Z||000007||NEPTUNE||12-27-2001||11:31 AM||||How manu dollars was the pump? Is impeller steel, plastic, or ? <BR>I'll be needing one soon........before summer. ||||reg||5
Z||000008||terryk||12-27-2001||11:42 AM||||I invariably wind up opening the coolant system so that the dex-cools life doesn't gain anything.<P>I'm not saying dex-cool isn't good, I just don't think it's worth putting in. Good green stuff and distilled water will work just fine. But hey, if you want dex-cool use it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero_Freak||12-27-2001||01:43 PM||||One thing with the metal impeller waterpump:<P>THEY SUCK!   That's right I said they suck.  And they do it very well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Mine has sucked up three thermostats.  Accually collapsed the whole thing.  It's not the brand of stat... Anything upwards of 5500-6000 rpm...and then temp starts dropping.  I know then that I've just sucked another one down.  <P>They are still way better than the plastic impellers though<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg">  <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000010||87fastback||12-27-2001||02:58 PM||||I used to work for GM and the only reason vehicles used Dex-cool is because of aluminum radiators, heads or waterpumps.  This coolant will not corrode aluminum as rapidly, but be sure if you switch to flush the system VERY good.  If you mix it in any amount it will goop up like sludge on you!  Take my word for it and stick with what you have.  You will see no benefits of this coolant other than longer water pump life, which if you weigh the cost of the coolant and time to flush - you will see it isn't worth the extra $$$.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000011||PBJ||12-27-2001||03:32 PM||||I don't think it is safe assumption to say dex cool is extended life-there are other types out there.  In my opinion,(and I am a GM tech) I would not put dexcool in my car, cause I think it is crappy sludge and GM got"taken" by fast talking sales people looking for another selling perk.<P>"I would not use dexcool in my car!"<P>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000012||Posthumane||12-27-2001||03:50 PM||||Ok, I'm not familiar with the name. Is Dex-cool a propelene glycol solution or something?||||reg||1
Z||000013||88CoupeV6||12-27-2001||05:11 PM||||Do a search on dex-cool on the internet. You will find a lot of sites that tell you the stuff is bad news. Plugs and gums everything up.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||12-27-2001||07:22 PM||||Read the stuff in my cave on coolant.....<P>GM's published position, the last one I saw, is that they will not extend coolant change intervals for older cars using Dex-Cool. The reports thru <A HREF="http://www.imcool.com" TARGET=_blank>www.imcool.com</A>  and other trade sites, by GM and Texaco, blame sludging and other problems with Dex-Cool on dealers, thirdparty shops, and DIYs. Their claim is that samples they've collected show systems are either contaminated or have too little Dex-Cool in them. Having seen how most people treat cooling systems I'm prone to believing them on this one.<P>I don't think that Dex-cool is a bad product but it appears to be allot more b_tchy about concentration and contamination. Since GM still says you should use the same service interval as green juice, why bother.... <P>The most important part with any coolant is making certain it's at the right concentration. That means a 50/50 mix in the majority of cars. (You can add more antifreeze than 50% but never less...)<P>I'm staying with a good brand of green coolant and maybe a bottle of Water Wetter. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000015||87GTZ34||12-28-2001||01:31 AM||||I've switched over the Dex-cool with no problems yet. It's only been 9 months and 2500 miles though.  I'm very particular about keeping things clean etc.  I've always used distilled water when adding or changing coolant (over 20 yrs).  When I changed this car over, I replaced the waterpump, thermostat and all of the hoses.  The radiator was replaced in '98 so I left it alone.  I pressure checked the system at max temp and had no leaks.  After that I flushed it over and over until the the flush water was as clear as the human can detect which turned out to be 7 times.  I'll let people know of my findings as I remove and check the system once per year.  I don't toss the old coolant until it is change time though. I just remove, inspect and put it back in if all is well.||||reg||1
Z||000016||87fastback||12-28-2001||01:47 AM||||I would really have to agree with PBJ on this one.  We just got finished replacing intake manifold gaskets on a Bravada that had Dex-cool that some lambrain decided to mix a little green stuff in as well.  This was run this way for 500 miles and it was like sludge coming out.  And considering this car was sold less than a month ago and I personally saw the coolant before hand (good clean Dex-cool), just a little bit of contamination with anything else and it turns to sludge.  Here is a list of damaged parts that were beyond repair: radiator, water pump, heads needed boiling as well as intake, thermostat, hmmmmm shall I go on!<P>After all was said and done this was a very hefty repair, all becaus of contamination.  All I can say is stick with what you have, you will save yourself in the long run as there is NO WAY to get all the regular anti-freeze out of the system to avoid contamination!<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
